# Language-specific assets live here as ordinary asset trees.
#
# The directory immediately below lang/ is the group name, and
# everything below that directory has the same layout it would have in
# the shared asset tree. So, for example, the French version of a
# localized string lives under:
#
#   lang/french/english/localizedstrings/
#
# The english/ component can look a little odd here. It is intentional.
# The Linker has no language switch of its own. Its localize loader
# always asks for english/localizedstrings/ and the parser always keeps
# the LANG_ENGLISH value of a reference. Selecting French therefore
# means putting French text where that lookup will find it.
#
# The build does this by prepending lang/<language>/ to the Linker's
# asset search path when it builds the group of that name. A file
# present in the overlay shadows the shared one and an absent file
# simply falls through to the shared asset tree. Note that this applies
# to any asset type, which is useful for the few retail zones where the
# language-specific difference is an image rather than a string.
#
# See doc/localization.md for the details of the Linker's language
# handling and why the source tree is arranged this way.
#
./: file{** -buildfile}

# These files are Linker inputs and have no useful installed form of
# their own. The resulting fastfiles carry the selected assets, so
# installing the overlay beside them would just copy build-time source
# data into the game directory.
#
file{** -buildfile}: install = false
